<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis VIII of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1187">1187</a>–<a href="/years/1226">1226</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Louis the Lion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis VIII, called the Lion, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1223 to 1226 and son of Philip II Augustus. As prince he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1216 at the invitation of rebel barons but was defeated at Lincoln in 1217. As king he completed the conquest of Poitou from the English in 1224 and led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Albigensians</a>. He began the practice of granting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> to royal sons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-viii-of-france.jpg" alt="Louis VIII of France" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Natalis de Wailly · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Isabella of Hainaut, daughter of Baldwin V, married Philip II Augustus of France in 1180 and was the mother of <b>Louis VIII</b>, so later French kings descended from the counts of Hainaut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The intervention of the French crown proved decisive: <b>Louis VIII</b>’s crusade of 1226 broke resistance, and by the Treaty of Paris of 1229 Raymond VII kept a reduced county for life, while his only daughter Joan was married to Alphonse of Poitiers, brother of Louis IX.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The French crown conquered Poitou between 1204 and 1224, when <b>Louis VIII</b> took La Rochelle, and Henry III's attempt to recover it failed at the Battle of Taillebourg in 1242.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The last count, William, bishop of Châlons, died in 1226, and <b>Louis VIII</b> and Louis IX absorbed the county into the royal domain of the Kingdom of France.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-perche">County of Perche</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip II seized Poitou from King John from 1204, and <b>Louis VIII</b> completed the conquest in 1224.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-poitou">County of Poitou</a></li></ul></section>
